# Optional -- copy to docker/.env (gitignored) and set a real value before
# first "docker compose up -d"; unset defaults to trust auth. To set or
# rotate it on an already-running cluster, use `pg-guard -set-password`
# instead (see README.md's "Setting a password later") -- editing this file
# alone has no effect without a restart, and can't cleanly rotate an
# already-set password anyway. Read by docker-compose.yml via
# ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-}. Both nodes' postgres user AND the replication role
# share this password (PG_GUARD_REPL_PASSWORD defaults to it; see
# ../README.md's Configuration table).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme

# Optional -- bearer token required on POST /api/* (promote/shutdown/
# rejoin/switchover/maintenance/start) if set; unset disables the check.
# GET /health/ready/status/metrics never require it. Both nodes must share
# the same value --
# requestPeerPromote (../src/peer.go) attaches it automatically when calling
# the peer's own /api/promote. See ../README.md's Authentication section.
PG_GUARD_API_TOKEN=changeme

# Optional -- switches the API listener from plain HTTP (:8080) to TLS
# (:8443) -- a real port change, not a silent same-port switch, so the
# port number always tells you which one you're hitting -- plus outbound
# peer calls and Postgres's own connections (pg-guard adds ssl_cert_file/
# ssl_key_file/ssl_ca_file automatically -- nothing else to set), once
# both are set; unset (the default) leaves today's plain-everything
# behavior on :8080 unchanged. Named after Postgres's own standard
# ssl_cert_file/ssl_key_file GUCs, not libpq's client-side PGSSLCERT/
# PGSSLKEY (a different thing -- see ../README.md's TLS section).
# Run ./generate-certs.sh first -- a local micro CA (10y) + leaf cert (2y)
# covering both node names + localhost -- these paths match its output,
# already mounted read-only into both containers at /run/secrets/tls (see
# docker-compose.yml).
# PG_GUARD_SSL_CERT_FILE=/run/secrets/tls/tls.crt
# PG_GUARD_SSL_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/tls/tls.key

# Optional -- CA trusted to verify an inbound client cert
# (PG_GUARD_MTLS_REQUIRE) and the peer's own cert on outbound calls. Point
# this at generate-certs.sh's CA (tls/ca.crt), not the leaf cert (tls.crt)
# -- the two are separate files here, unlike a single self-signed cert.
# PG_GUARD_SSL_CA_FILE=/run/secrets/tls/ca.crt

# Optional -- require and verify a client certificate on every inbound
# POST /api/* connection (mutual TLS), enforced at the TLS handshake
# before any request handler runs. Requires PG_GUARD_SSL_CERT_FILE/PG_GUARD_SSL_KEY_FILE/
# PG_GUARD_SSL_CA_FILE all set above.
# PG_GUARD_MTLS_REQUIRE=true

# Optional -- for genuinely extra postgres args unrelated to TLS (TLS
# itself is switched on automatically above, not through this).
# PG_GUARD_EXTRA_ARGS=

# --- Multi-host production deployment (planned, not yet built here) ---
#
# This .env.example is for docker-compose.yml's co-located two-node TEST
# stack, where PG_GUARD_PEER_HOST is auto-derived from each container's
# -0/-1 hostname suffix (see ../src/config.go's derivePeerHost) -- Docker
# Compose's embedded DNS resolves that name within the shared network, so
# nothing needs setting below for that.
#
# A real deployment puts each node on its own separate host, with no
# shared Docker network -- there, PG_GUARD_PEER_HOST must be set
# explicitly instead, to the OTHER host's real, externally-resolvable
# address. Each host's own local .env would set this to point at its
# peer -- named by ordinal (-0/-1), not by role, since role is runtime
# state that flips on switchover/failover; a host permanently named after
# "primary"/"standby" would be wrong as soon as that happens. E.g. on the
# -0 host:
#   PG_GUARD_PEER_HOST=pg-guard-1.example.internal
# and on the -1 host, the reverse. Same identical single-service compose
# file on both hosts; only this value differs per host.
